  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x^2 + 0.4 z^2) / 2, drawn at half its value as height. x (left to right) is the steep direction. Plain steps there grow without limit above a learning rate of 2/2.4 = 0.83.",
    "Each step: settings -= learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is random, centred on zero, with size 1.2 × sqrt((64 − batch) / (63 × batch)) per direction, so it is exactly 0 at batch 64. Noise comes from the fixed seed, run and step.",
    "Both arrows are 0.6 × a slope, on one scale. The purple arrow points against the noisy mini-batch slope, which is the way the ball moves. The dark arrow points against the true slope. Both are hidden once the run ends.",
    "Diverged: the loss goes above 5, or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. Points off the bowl are drawn at its rim, but the readouts keep the true values. Otherwise a run is finished after 40 steps.",
    "A step lowers the loss only on average. With small batches, a single step can raise it. The trail holds the start plus up to 40 steps, and the ghost keeps the last run that took at least one step."
  ]
}